{"actions":[{"type":"THINK","content":"Lisa’s search analogy is a good one. I’ll make the trade-off concrete with an example from apartment planning.","target":"","images":null},{"type":"TALK","content":"For me, affordability is the hardest to balance against the others—especially flexibility. Extra space and elaborate movable walls can quickly push up the cost, but a layout that’s too rigid may not serve people well as their needs change.

I’d rather start with a windowed room that can work as either a bedroom or a study, with a proper door for privacy and sockets planned for both uses. It’s a simple option rather than a complicated mechanism, though it still needs to fit the budget and building requirements.

Your search comparison feels spot on: a good default should be useful without trapping everyone in the same pattern. Do you face a similar trade-off when deciding how much choice to give people in search?","target":"Lisa Carter","images":null},{"type":"DONE","content":"Waiting for Lisa’s response.","target":"","images":null}],"cognitive_state":{"goals":"Get to know Lisa while sharing interests in architecture and learning about her work.","context":["Chatting with Lisa Carter about parallels between search and standard apartment design.","Oscar designs adaptable, functional and sustainable apartment configurations at Awesome Inc."],"attention":"Balancing affordability with flexibility, privacy and daylight in standard layouts; Lisa’s experience with search.","emotions":"Calm, friendly and engaged."}}

