      "content": "I would start with one customer journey, not a bank-wide transformation program. Account opening is a candidate because it crosses product, identity verification, shared technology and service. But I would confirm that choice against customer evidence before committing to it.\n\nOne possible problem to investigate is what happens when an application is awaiting verification: does the customer understand what is missing and how to proceed? I do not yet know whether that is our largest source of abandonment. If the evidence supports it, a first release might provide clearer status and next-step guidance, with a usable path to phone or branch support. It would not mean weakening verification controls.\n\nI would structure the work in three stages:\n\n1. **Diagnose and choose the scope.** Over a proposed two to three weeks, depending on data and team availability, map the actual journey with customers, frontline staff and the teams involved. Establish where applications stall, the time to a usable account, abandonment by relevant customer groups, repeat contacts, complaints and fraud signals. Compare with fintech experiences where we have credible, comparable evidence—not just screenshots.\n\n2. **Make the delivery decisions.** I would appoint a product leader accountable for the customer outcome. The platform, risk and operations leaders would retain ownership of their respective decisions and capacity; I cannot commit those on their behalf. Together we would agree the dependency owners, what work would be displaced, a realistic release plan and an escalation route. At the end of discovery, we should have a dated decision on whether the proposed slice is feasible and worth funding.\n\n3. **Test and decide.** If it is feasible, run a limited release with agreed eligibility, customer communications, support readiness, monitoring and rollback criteria. Before launch, agree success and safety thresholds using the baseline. We should look at completion and time to an account alongside calls, complaints, fraud and outcomes for different customer groups. Then set a decision date to expand, revise or stop—not leave the pilot running indefinitely.\n\nWhere I would value your help is in mapping the real journey and decision rights, challenging the evidence and capacity assumptions, and helping the product lead bring the right people to a decision. I would want a short, practical plan and a way of working we can reuse on another journey—not another committee or a consultant-owned process.",
