{"actions":[{"type":"THINK","content":"The problems we named share a theme: important information is incomplete, ambiguous, or changes over time. I should suggest a substantial Word concept that makes those uncertainties visible, with examples relevant to our different fields, and avoid presenting AI as an unquestioned authority.","target":"" , "images":null},{"type":"TALK","content":"My strongest idea is a **living, evidence-aware document**. Word would understand a document as more than paragraphs: it would recognize claims, requirements, decisions, and how they depend on related material. A reader could see what source supports a statement, where sources disagree, and which parts are assumptions or uncertain. If a source or requirement changes, Word could point out the sections that may need review rather than silently rewriting them.

That could matter in architecture when specifications depend on regulations or customer requirements, and in clinical documentation when notes contain conflicting or incomplete information. The important part is that the AI exposes uncertainty and leaves consequential decisions to the person responsible.

A second, complementary idea is a **reader stress test**: Word shows where different readers could interpret a passage differently, what context they may be missing, and which questions the document leaves unanswered. I’d prioritize the evidence-aware document, because it addresses not just clearer writing but the reliability and continuity of the decisions a document carries.","target":"","images":null},{"type":"DONE","content":"Waiting for others to build on these ideas.","target":"" ,"images":null}],"cognitive_state":{"goals":"Contribute and develop promising AI feature ideas for Microsoft Word, focusing on workplace and industry productivity.","context":["I am a data scientist on Microsoft’s M365 Search team, familiar with noisy and biased data, ambiguous queries, relevance measurement, privacy constraints, and AI reliability concerns.","The group includes an architect who described regulatory, customer, cost, and environmental pressures, and a neurologist who described uncertainty in clinical work and documentation constraints.","The discussion has moved from identifying problems to brainstorming ambitious AI features for Word; solutions are now requested."],"attention":"An evidence-aware document that makes claims, sources, conflicts, assumptions, and downstream dependencies visible; a complementary reader stress-test for ambiguity.","emotions":"Calm, engaged, and professionally curious."}}

