      "type": "TALK",
      "content": "I see two promising directions:\n\n1. **Word as a living evidence lab.** A team starts with a question, such as whether a new search ranking actually helps users. Word turns the document into a workspace linking claims to authorized data, examples and tests. It could surface ambiguous queries, overlooked user groups and competing explanations, then create cases for human review. As evidence changes, the team can see which conclusions are affected and where uncertainty remains.\n\n2. **Word as a decision rehearsal space.** A document becomes an interactive model of goals, constraints and alternatives. People could change assumptions and explore consequences across text, tables and sketches. For apartment design, that might mean examining how a different budget or accessibility need affects a configuration. For a care plan, it could make uncertainties and family priorities visible for discussion. The document would distinguish established evidence from AI-generated possibilities rather than presenting a scenario as a professional decision.\n\nBoth ideas make Word more than a place to record a conclusion: it becomes a place to examine how we reached it. Which direction should we develop first?",
